<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-period-frequency" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Period and Frequency</title>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       EXAMPLE: 10 kg mass, k = 140
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    <!-- PROSE: quick callback to the setup on the previous page, something like
         "let's put that ODE to work with a concrete example" -->
  </p>

  <p>
    Recall that an undamped mass-spring system satisfies
    <me>mx'' + kx = 0, \qquad \text{i.e.} \qquad x'' + \omega_0^2 x = 0,</me>
    where <m>\omega_0 = \sqrt{k/m}</m>.
  </p>

  <example xml:id="ex-spring-10kg">
    <title>A 10 kg Mass on a Spring</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        A block of mass <m>m = 10</m> kg is attached to a spring with spring
        constant <m>k = 140</m> N/m. Find the general solution <m>x(t)</m>
        describing its position.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <hint>
      <p>
        Plug <m>m = 10</m> and <m>k = 140</m> into <m>mx'' + kx = 0</m> and solve.
      </p>
    </hint>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Substituting <m>m = 10</m> and <m>k = 140</m>:
        <me>10x'' + 140x = 0.</me>
        Dividing through by 10:
        <me>x'' + 14x = 0.</me>
        So <m>\omega_0^2 = 14</m>, giving <m>\omega_0 = \sqrt{14}</m>.
        The general solution is:
        <me>x(t) = c_1\cos\!\left(\sqrt{14}\, t\right) + c_2\sin\!\left(\sqrt{14}\, t\right).</me>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       INTERACTIVE: spring animation fixed to omega = sqrt(14)
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <figure xml:id="fig-spring-sqrt14">
    <caption>
      The solution <m>x(t) = c_1\cos(\sqrt{14}\,t) + c_2\sin(\sqrt{14}\,t)</m>
      for our 10 kg mass. The left panel shows the spring; the right panel
      traces position over time. The shaded blue region marks
      <em>one full oscillation</em>. 
    </caption>
    <interactive xml:id="interactive-spring-sqrt14"
      iframe="ch3-graph/interactive-spring-sqrt14.html"
      width="100%" aspect="5:3">
      <description>
        An animated horizontal mass-spring system with a position versus time
        graph. The natural frequency is fixed at omega naught equals square
        root of 14. Sliders control c1 and c2, the coefficients of the cosine
        and sine terms. A shaded blue region on the graph marks one complete
        oscillation. A red dot traces the current position on the curve. No
        matter how c1 and c2 are adjusted, the width of the shaded region
        (the period) stays the same.
      </description>
    </interactive>
  </figure>

  <p>
    Notice that no matter what you set c1 and c2 to,
         the shaded region (one full oscillation) always has the same width.
         The shape of the curve changes, but the time it takes to complete one
         cycle doesn't. That time is what we call the period."
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       PERIOD: informal intro
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    The period is just how long one full oscillation takes.
         for our spring, it's the time from, say, maximum stretch back around
         to maximum stretch again.
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       INTERACTIVE: period explorer, cos(omega t) with omega slider
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <figure xml:id="fig-period-explorer">
    <caption>
      The function <m>x(t) = 2\cos(\omega_0 t)</m> for different values of
      <m>\omega_0</m>. The shaded region shows one full period, with the
      period value updating live as you move the slider. What's the
      relationship between <m>\omega_0</m> and the period?
    </caption>
    <interactive xml:id="interactive-period-explorer"
      iframe="ch3-graph/interactive-period-explorer.html"
      width="100%" aspect="5:3">
      <description>
        A graph of 2 cosine of omega naught times t. A blue shaded region
        marks one full period, bounded by red dashed vertical lines. A label
        shows the numerical value of the period. A slider controls omega
        naught from 0.5 to 4. As omega naught increases, the curve oscillates
        faster and the shaded period region gets narrower. The period equals
        2 pi divided by omega naught.
      </description>
    </interactive>
  </figure>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       FREQUENCY + OBSERVATION BLOCK
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    A related quantity is frequency: how many
         full oscillations happen per unit time. bigger omega means faster
         oscillation, so higher frequency. the two are just reciprocals.
  </p>

  <observation xml:id="obs-period-frequency">
    <title>Period and Frequency of Simple Harmonic Motion</title>
    <p>
      For simple harmonic motion <m>x(t) = c_1\cos(\omega_0 t) + c_2\sin(\omega_0 t)</m>:
      <ul>
        <li><p>
          The <term>period</term> (time to complete one full oscillation) is:
          <me>T = \frac{2\pi}{\omega_0}.</me>
        </p></li>
        <li><p>
          The <term>frequency</term> (number of oscillations per unit time) is:
          <me>f = \frac{\omega_0}{2\pi} = \frac{1}{T}.</me>
        </p></li>
      </ul>
      Larger <m>\omega_0</m> means faster oscillation: shorter period, higher frequency.
    </p>
  </observation>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       MC CHECKPOINT
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mc-period-freq">
    <statement>
      <p>
        For the 10 kg mass-spring system from <xref ref="ex-spring-10kg"/>,
        where <m>\omega_0 = \sqrt{14}</m>, which of the following correctly
        gives the period <m>T</m> and frequency <m>f</m>?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="yes">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p><m>T = \dfrac{2\pi}{\sqrt{14}}</m> and <m>f = \dfrac{\sqrt{14}}{2\pi}</m></p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Exactly right. With <m>\omega_0 = \sqrt{14}</m>, the period is
            <m>T = 2\pi/\sqrt{14} \approx 1.68</m> seconds, and the frequency
            is <m>f = \sqrt{14}/(2\pi) \approx 0.60</m> oscillations per second.
            <m>\checkmark</m>
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p><m>T = \dfrac{2\pi}{\sqrt{14}}</m> and <m>f = \dfrac{2\pi}{\sqrt{14}}</m></p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            The period is correct, but frequency and period are reciprocals of
            each other, not equal. Frequency is <m>f = 1/T = \sqrt{14}/(2\pi)</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p><m>T = \sqrt{14}</m> and <m>f = \dfrac{1}{\sqrt{14}}</m></p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            The period formula is <m>T = 2\pi/\omega_0</m>, not just
            <m>\omega_0</m> itself. The <m>2\pi</m> comes from the fact that
            cosine completes one full cycle over an interval of length <m>2\pi</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p><m>T = \dfrac{\pi}{\sqrt{14}}</m> and <m>f = \dfrac{\sqrt{14}}{\pi}</m></p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Close, but off by a factor of 2. One full oscillation of cosine
            spans <m>2\pi</m>, not <m>\pi</m> -- that would only be a half-cycle.
            The correct period is <m>T = 2\pi/\sqrt{14}</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       KEY TAKEAWAYS
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <conclusion>
    <title>Key Takeaways</title>
    <p><ul>
      <li><p>
        <alert>Period:</alert> <m>T = 2\pi/\omega_0</m> -- the time for one
        complete oscillation. Depends only on <m>\omega_0 = \sqrt{k/m}</m>,
        not on how far you pull the mass.
      </p></li>
      <li><p>
        <alert>Frequency:</alert> <m>f = \omega_0/(2\pi) = 1/T</m> --
        oscillations per unit time. Larger <m>\omega_0</m> means higher frequency
        and shorter period.
      </p></li>
      <li><p>
        <alert>Initial conditions don't affect period:</alert> changing <m>c_1</m>
        and <m>c_2</m> changes the shape of the oscillation (amplitude, starting
        position) but not how fast it oscillates.
      </p></li>
    </ul></p>
  </conclusion>

